> I think too often when we find a problem with a piece of software,
> we label it "crap," which might be appropriate if we plunked down
> tons of $$ for it.  But here, there are people working on their own
> time to provide us with (in general, damn good) software for free.

I agree for the most part, but sometimes it absolutely kills me to
see an otherwise marvelous piece of software with a few fatal flaws
that could have been VERY, VERY easily fixed.

I think that's how many people feel about PC NetHack.  The dev team
got all the features in, it runs like a charm, it is a marvelous piece
of programming, a testament to the net...BUT I CAN'T SEE THE DAMN
WALLS!!!  AAAARGH!  ;-)

*I* didn't have that problem, but I could see how someone who did
and didn't know how to fix it (I do) could get very frustrated.
If the game were a piece of crap, they probably wouldn't care, but
they see that it works very well but is almost impossible for them
to use because of a minor, easily fixed, design glitch.

No, there isn't any place for rudeness, but if *I* released a program
that I'd worked like crazy to write, I would want to know if people
were finding some minor feature of it impossible to live with.


